course: chemistry
lesson: 2
title: Elements and the famous members of the periodic table
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An element is a substance made of only one kind of atom: gold is only gold atoms, oxygen only oxygen atoms.
explain: The big chart of coloured squares on the science-classroom wall is the periodic table, and every square is one element.
explain: Scientists put the elements in order of how many protons their atoms have, and elements that behave alike end up in the same column, like families.
explain: Everything you know, from a gold ring to a fizzy-drink can to the air you breathe, is made of elements alone or joined together.
explain: Think of the elements as the letters of an alphabet: about a hundred letters spell out every substance in the world.
fact: There are 118 known elements; about 90 are found in nature and the rest were made in laboratories, some lasting less than a second.
fact: Sodium is a soft metal that fizzes wildly in water and chlorine is a poisonous gas, but joined together they make a new, safe substance: ordinary table salt.
fact: Elements in the periodic table are in order of the number of protons in their atoms.
fact: Gold hardly reacts with air or water, so gold from ancient Egyptian tombs still shines while iron from the same time has rusted away.
fact: Only about one fifth of the air is oxygen; most of the rest is nitrogen, which we breathe in and out unchanged.
fact: Our bodies use oxygen to get energy from food, and fires need oxygen too: with more oxygen, things burn much more fiercely.
fact: Diamond and pencil graphite are both pure carbon; the atoms are arranged differently, a strong 3-D cage in diamond and sliding flat sheets in graphite; coal is mostly carbon too, so in a very hot fire a diamond can burn like coal.
fact: Helium is lighter than air, so helium balloons float; it was first found in the Sun's light, and its name comes from the Greek for "Sun".
fact: Breathing helium from a gas tank is dangerous: it can harm the lungs and leaves the body without oxygen.
fact: Mercury is the only metal that is liquid at room temperature; it is poisonous, so a broken mercury thermometer is a job for a grown-up; in very hard frost, below about minus 39 degrees, it freezes into a solid metal.
fact: Most drink cans are aluminium, a light metal protected by a thin invisible layer that stops it wearing away.
fact: Long ago aluminium was so hard to get out of rock that it was more precious than gold.
fact: Neon gas glows orange-red when electricity passes through it; each element glows with its own colours.
fact: Iron in red blood cells helps carry oxygen around the body; meat, beans and lentils are good sources, and spinach is not as iron-rich as the old story says.
fact: Each element has a short symbol: Na for sodium, Cl for chlorine, Fe for iron, Au for gold; some come from old Latin names, like Fe from ferrum, the Latin word for iron.
exercise: What is the big chart of coloured squares on the science-classroom wall? | The periodic table of the elements | A map of the world's metal mines | A chart of the planets | A list of all the molecules
exercise: What is an element? | A substance made of only one kind of atom | Two metals melted together | A substance that is always a shiny solid | One of earth, air, fire and water
exercise: Why does a gold ring stay shiny for thousands of years? | Gold hardly reacts with air or water | Gold is polished by the skin every day | Gold is covered in a clear paint | Gold atoms are too heavy to change
exercise: Which part of the air do we need to get energy from food? | Oxygen | Nitrogen | Helium gas | Neon gas
exercise: Most of the air we breathe in is which gas? | Nitrogen | Pure oxygen | Carbon dioxide | Helium gas
exercise: A pencil and a diamond are made of the same element. Which one? | Carbon | Pure silver | Clear glass | Lead metal
exercise: Why does a helium balloon float up to the ceiling? | Helium is lighter than the air around it | Helium is pulled up towards the Sun | The balloon rubber is lighter than air | Warm air from people pushes it up
exercise: What is the silver liquid in an old thermometer? | Mercury, a metal that is liquid at room temperature | Melted silver kept warm inside the glass | Water with silver paint mixed in so that you can see it clearly | Liquid aluminium from a can
exercise: What are most fizzy-drink cans made of? | Aluminium | Thin sheet iron | Tin and nothing else | Gold-coloured plastic
exercise: What makes a neon sign glow red? | Electricity makes the neon atoms give out light | A red bulb hidden away behind the long glass tube | The neon gas is burning slowly | Red paint glowing in the dark
exercise: Why does your blood need iron? | It helps red blood cells carry oxygen | It makes your bones hard and strong | It keeps your blood warm in winter | It makes your blood taste salty
exercise: On a science poster, what does "Na" stand for? | Sodium | Nitrogen | Neon gas | Nickel metal
exercise: What do sodium and chlorine make when they join together? | Ordinary table salt | White sugar | Pure water | Baking soda
exercise: About how many elements are found in nature? | About 90 | Only about 4 | About 1,000 | Roughly 20
test: Your cousin says: "Fe on that poster is short for Fe-iron, obviously." What is the real reason for Fe? | It comes from the old Latin name for iron, ferrum | It is short for "feather metal", because iron is so light | It stands for "fixed element" | Iron was first found by a scientist called Fe
test: "why'd anyone make wedding rings outta gold and not iron, iron's way cheaper innit" Best answer? | Gold stays shiny for ages, iron would rust | Iron is far too heavy to wear on a finger all day | Gold is the only metal that can be bent | Iron rings are against the law
test: In a game, a character breathes helium from a big gas tank and just talks squeaky. What is wrong? | Breathing from a tank can harm the lungs and starve you of oxygen | Nothing is wrong, it is a safe trick for everyone, even from a big tank | Helium would make the character float away | Helium would set the character on fire
test: Your friend says: "Spinach is the best food for iron by miles, that's why Popeye is strong." Put him right. | That story is exaggerated; meat, beans and lentils are as good or better | He is right, spinach is the only food in the world that has any iron in it | Spinach contains no iron at all | Iron only comes from tablets
test: What would happen if you cooled mercury to minus 50 degrees? | It would freeze into a solid metal | It would boil away and turn into a gas | It would stay liquid forever | It would turn into silver
test: Sodium fizzes violently in water and chlorine is a poisonous gas. Why is table salt safe? | Joined together they make a new substance with new behaviour | Salt has only a tiny bit of each, too little to hurt | The poison is boiled out in the factory before the salt is packed | Salt is not really made of sodium and chlorine
test: A diamond is thrown into a very hot furnace. What could happen? | It can burn, because it is just carbon like coal | Nothing at all, because diamonds can never change in any way | It would turn into gold | It would melt into glass
test: "pff my mate says helium was found in a cave in Texas first" What is the truth? | It was first found in the Sun's light, before it was found on Earth | It was first found at the bottom of the sea | He is right, it is named after Texas | It was first made in a lab in a school, by children doing an experiment
test: If oxygen made up all the air instead of a fifth, what would be the danger? | Things would burn much more fiercely | Nobody could breathe at all | Fires would be impossible | All the balloons would float away
test: Your sister says: "The periodic table is just in alphabetical order." Put her right. | It is in order of the number of protons in each atom | She is right, it starts with A and ends with Z | It is in order of colour | It is in order of who discovered them first, from the oldest to the newest
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a chemical element? | A substance composed exclusively of atoms of a single kind | A substance composed of several metals in combination | A substance invariably solid at ordinary temperatures | A substance found only in laboratories
exam: Identify the principal reason for which gold retains its lustre over millennia. | Its negligible reactivity with atmospheric oxygen and with water | The continual polishing it receives from its owners | Its concealment beneath a transparent protective lacquer applied by goldsmiths | The exceptional mass of its constituent atoms
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning diamond and graphite. | Both consist solely of carbon, the arrangement of the atoms differing | Diamond consists of carbon, whereas graphite consists of the metal lead | Both consist of silicon in differing quantities | Graphite consists of carbon whereas diamond consists of glass
exam: It is given that the atmosphere is inhaled. Ascertain the constituent present in the greatest proportion. | Nitrogen | Oxygen | Carbon dioxide | Helium
